// D2 release property, written with a `&mut` capture. It pins that a FIELD loan
// whose holder is still live blocks the write — the same property as the shared
// twin, reached through the mut spelling.
//
// ⚠ IT DOES NOT PIN MUTNESS, AND THE HEADER USED TO SAY IT DID. Measured
// 2026-08-25: a `&mut` closure capture of a FIELD registers a SHARED loan —
// `capture_is_mut` never reaches the field record — so this file and its shared
// twin `bc_nll_d2_field_live_refuse` pin the SAME string, and it is the
// shared-borrow wording (`'s.a' is already borrowed`, not `is already mutably
// borrowed`). Perturbing the mutness makes neither file red.
//
// The hole that hides behind it is live and filed as D6:
//     two mut-capturing closures of the SAME field, both live      -> rc 0
//     the byte-identical program over the WHOLE variable           -> rc 1
//         "cannot borrow 'n' as mutable: already mutably borrowed"
// So `|| { s.a = s.a + 1 }` does not block a concurrent read of `s.a`, where the
// whole-var and the direct `&mut s.a` spellings both do. When D6 closes, this
// file's `.expected` must move to the MUTABLY-borrowed wording, and that change
// is the proof the fix landed.
package bc_nll_d2_field_live_mut_refuse;
struct S { a: i64, b: i64 }
fn main() -> i64 {
    let mut s: S = S { a: 1i64, b: 2i64 };
    let mut c = || { s.a = s.a + 1i64; };
    s.a = 5i64;                       // must be REFUSED: `c` is used below
    c();
    return s.a;
}
